      "claim": "The Fourth Geneva Convention applies de facto to the territories east of the 1949 Armistice Line occupied by Israel in 1967, irrespective of whether Jordan was recognised as the sovereign prior to occupation.",
      "reasoning": "Article 2(2) of the Convention states that it applies to all cases of ‘occupation of the territory of a High Contracting Party’. International legal practice interprets ‘territory of a High Contracting Party’ to include territories that were under the de jure jurisdiction of a High Contracting Party, even if that jurisdiction was not exercised as full sovereignty. Jordan, as a party to the Convention, exercised de jure authority over the West Bank under the 1949 Armistice Agreement, and thus the occupied territories fell within the scope of the Convention. This interpretation has been consistently affirmed in the jurisprudence of the International Court of Justice and the International Committee of the Red Cross, which treat the Convention’s protections as triggered by the fact of occupation by a High Contracting Party, not by the existence of a prior recognized sovereign state.",
      "citations": []
    },
